// ADR-127: a pipeline's source is the `for` loop's, and a collection converts
// by naming what it becomes.
//
// Every line below was `Y110` — "no method `map` on type `Set[Int]`" — before
// the pipeline's receiver became the generic `Iterable`. The gap was never a
// missing feature per collection: `capability::iter_item` has answered "what can
// I iterate and what does it yield" for eleven collections plus `Text` since
// M8-WS6, and §6.3's combinators were registered against one receiver.
//
// So this program is the ten receivers, once each, plus the conversions that let
// a chain end anywhere it started.
//
// It used to be split into six functions, and said so here: "It is split into
// sections because one function's `Gc` locals are bounded by `MAX_SHADOW_SLOTS`,
// not because the sections mean anything on their own." That is no longer true
// and the split is gone (ADR-128). `main` has 340 `Gc` locals and **seven**
// simultaneously-live roots, so it claims seven shadow slots; the cap it used to
// hit was never a statement about this program, only about how its slots were
// numbered. The output is byte-identical to the six-function form's, which is
// what says the merge is a merge and not a rewrite.

// The five sequence-shaped receivers, plus the two nullary ones.
var s = Set()
s.insert(3)
s.insert(1)
s.insert(2)
// A `Set` has no member accessor in the language at all — `praxis_set_items`
// was reachable only from `for` — so this is the first spelling that reads
// one. The order is `maps::ordered_members`', which is deterministic and
// seed-independent: `s.map(f)` answers the same `Vec` on every run.
out(s.map(|x| x * 2).sum())
out(s.sorted())

var d = Deque()
d.push_back(10)
d.push_front(20)
out(d.map(|x| x + 1).sum())

var heap = MinHeap()
heap.push(5)
heap.push(2)
// A heap's backing array is heap-ordered only at its root, which is why
// reading one through `praxis_vec_get` was a silently wrong answer and why
// `IterPlan` exists. The pipeline takes the same snapshot a `for` does.
out(heap.count())

var bits = BitSet()
bits.insert(1)
bits.insert(4)
out(bits.map(|n| n * n).sum())

out((0..10).filter(|x| x % 2 == 0).map(|x| x * 2).sum())

// `Text` is the one iterable scalar, and it yields the `Char` that `t[i]`
// already answers.
out("hello".count(|c| c == "l"[0]))
out("hello".filter(|c| c != "l"[0]).count())

// A `Map` and a `Counter` yield **pairs**, from two index-aligned snapshots the
// loop joins into the `(K, V)` tuple the item type names.
var m = Map()
m["a"] = 1
m["b"] = 2
out(m.count())
out(m.map(|kv| kv.1).sum())

var tally = ["the", "cat", "the", "dog", "the", "cat"].frequencies()
out(tally.filter(|p| p.1 > 1).count())

// **A pipeline's currency is `Vec`.** Every streaming stage answers one,
// whatever the receiver was, and a program that wants a collection back says
// which one. One sentence answers "what does `filter` return" for all ten
// receivers, and it is answerable without knowing which receiver you are on.
var s = Set()
s.insert(3)
s.insert(1)
s.insert(2)
out(s.filter(|x| x > 1))
out(s.filter(|x| x > 1).to_set().len())
// A fused sink, not a barrier: `v.map(f).to_set()` is one loop and no
// intermediate `Vec`.
out([1, 2, 3].map(|x| x % 2).to_set().len())

// `to_vec` is the only route out of a keyed collection: `keys()` and `values()`
// answer two aligned halves and nothing joins them. `to_map` is the route back
// in, and it says "a pair" in its receiver pattern rather than in prose — so
// `[1, 2].to_map()` is a type error at the method name and not a row that
// resolves and then faults.
var m = Map()
m["a"] = 1
m["b"] = 2
out(m.to_vec())

var doubled = m.map(|kv| (kv.0, kv.1 * 10)).to_map()
out(doubled["b"])

// The four whose only cost was a table entry apiece. Leaving them out is
// what would have made `deque.filter(p)` answer a `Vec` with no way back.
out([3, 1, 2].to_deque().pop_front())
out([3, 1, 2].to_min_heap().pop())
out([3, 1, 2].to_max_heap().pop())
out([1, 4].to_bitset().contains(4))

// `to_vec` on a `Vec` receiver is the identity and answers the **same
// reference**, not a copy — the second half of ADR-126 decision 2 kept.
var v = [1]
var same = v.to_vec()
same.push(2)
out(v.len())

// **A keyed collection cannot order its own items.** No composite is orderable
// (ADR-045), so the moment a pipeline's item is a pair — the moment its source
// is a `Map` or a `Counter` — `sorted` is unavailable and "the two most common
// words" has no spelling. The closure extracts an orderable key from an item
// that is not.
var tally = ["the", "cat", "the", "dog", "the", "cat"].frequencies()
var top = tally.to_vec().sorted_by_key(|p| 0 - p.1).take(2)
out(top)
out(top.to_counter()["the"])

// The `Ord` bound moved to the extracted key, so the elements need no order
// of their own — and the receiver is a `Set`, which a barrier could not take
// before decision 3.
var names = Set()
names.insert("bb")
names.insert("a")
names.insert("ccc")
out(names.sorted_by_key(|t| t.len()))

// The two barriers added after this program was written, over the same
// spread of receivers. `reversed` reads no descriptor callback (ADR-145), so
// it is the barrier with no bound on its element; `join` bounds its item to
// `Text` and answers a scalar (ADR-144).
out(v.reversed())
out(s.reversed())
out((0..5).reversed())
out("abc".reversed())
out(names.join("-"))
out(["a", "b", "c"].join(", "))
out(tally.keys().join("|"))
out([1, 2, 3].map(|n| n.to_text()).join("+"))

// A countdown, which before `reversed` was a `while` and a decrement.
var down = 0
for y in (0..5).reversed() { down = down * 10 + y }
out(down)

// `Vec[Char]` is the one sequence that becomes a `Text` under a different
// name, because `join` could not carry a second item bound (ADR-144).
out("héllo".to_vec().to_text())
